{{also|omerta}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|omerta}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|omertà|notext=1}}, Southern dialectal variant of {{m|it|umiltà||humility}}, from {{der|en|la|humilitās}}, from {{m|la|humilis||humble}}, from {{m|la|humus||ground, soil}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{wikipedia}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌəʊmɛːˈtɑː/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/oʊˈmɛɹtə/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɑː|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|crime}} {{l|en|A [[code of silence]] amongst members of a criminal organization (especially the [[Mafia]]) that forbids [[divulge|divulging]] insider secrets to [[law enforcement]], often also followed outside of the organization in fear of [[retaliation]]; extensively, any code of silence.}}
#* {{quote-news
|date=March 4, 2005
|work=Boston Globe
|passage=Patriarca pleaded guilty in December 1991 to racketeering and conspiracy charges, but he refused to admit he was a member of the Mafia, clinging to his vow of ‘'''omerta'''’ to the secret organization.
}}
#* {{quote-news
|date=October 27, 2006
|work=Los Angeles Times
|passage=There was a time that high-profile killings such as the 1968 assassinations of Robert F. Kennedy and Martin Luther King Jr. brought passionate cries for limitations on handguns. A bipartisan '''omerta''' now smothers the issue.
}}
#* {{quote-news
|date=October 2016
|url=http://www.scientificamerican.com/article/how-the-fda-manipulates-the-media/
|title=How the FDA Manipulates the Media
|work=Scientific American
|passage=The FDA was not pleased that the '''omertà''' had been broken.
}}

====Coordinate terms====
* {{l|en|wall of silence}}

